INT. STATION WAGON - HIGHWAY - DAY

Windows down. Wind roars. A cooler wedged between two kids in the back seat.

MATEUS FERRARI-OKONJO, forties, drives with one hand. His wife ILSE, forties, navigates with a paper map. In the back, TEODORA, fourteen, scowls at her phone; BLAISE, eleven, kicks the seat; and baby NANDI, one, gums a rubber giraffe.

BLAISE
Are we there yet?

MATEUS
No.

ILSE
It's another forty minutes.

BLAISE
You said that an hour ago.

TEODORA
Stop kicking.

He kicks harder. She slaps his knee. He yelps. The baby shrieks. Mateus turns the radio up.

ILSE
Turn that down.

MATEUS
It's the good part.

ILSE
It's the same part it's always been.

TEODORA
Dad, he's kicking me.

MATEUS
Blaise.

BLAISE
She started it.

ILSE
Mateus, the exit.

He swerves. Brakes squeal. The cooler thumps. Nandi howls. A truck blasts its horn.

MATEUS
Sorry. Sorry.

ILSE
You missed it.

MATEUS
I did not.

ILSE
You did.

TEODORA
(to Blaise, low)
I'm telling Mom you broke the cooler.

BLAISE
I did not!

The giraffe flies across the car and hits the windshield. Silence. The baby laughs.
